@meta
id: 13-01
title: The dot is not a dot
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-classes
par: 36
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+F, watch it land on the SQL, press Enter again
open: refund.service.ts

@brief
The refund argument type was renamed a while ago: its `total` became
`amount`. One call site in `submit` never caught up and still reads
`refund.total`. Make it `refund.amount`.

Search for `refund.total` and watch where the cursor lands. It is not on
the TypeScript. It is on `refund_total`, a SQL column name in a query
string twenty lines higher, and pressing `n` walks you through three
more of those before you reach the code.

That is the whole chapter in one line: what you type after `/` is not
text, it is a pattern, and in a pattern the dot means "any single
character". `refund.total` matches `refund_total`, `refund-total` and
`refund total` just as happily as the thing you meant. When you mean a
real dot, you have to say so.

@keys
| `.` | in a pattern: any one character |
| `\.` | a literal full stop |
| `/pattern/e` `Enter` | land on the last character of the match |
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |

@hints
The plain search lands on the SQL because the dot in your pattern
accepts the underscore. Tell vim the dot is a dot.
--
A backslash in front of a character that would otherwise be special
makes it literal. `\.` matches only a full stop.
--
`/refund\.total/e` `Enter` puts the cursor on the last letter of the
right `total`. `ciwamount` and `Esc` replaces the word.

@solution
/refund\.total/e<Enter>  ciwamount<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-one keystrokes, one of which is the backslash that made the
difference.

Everything in the search prompt is a pattern. Most characters stand for
themselves, which is why chapter 12 never had to mention it, but a
handful do not: `.`, `*`, `[`, `^`, `$`, `~` and `\` itself. When one of
those turns up in the text you are hunting for, put a backslash in front
of it. `\.` for a full stop, `\*` for an asterisk, `\[` for a bracket.
Paths like `order.repository` and version numbers like `2.4.1` are the
usual victims — `2.4.1` matches `2x4x1` and `2.401`.

The other direction is the useful one. Once you know `.` matches
anything, you can lean on it. `/refund.total` was wrong here because the
file happened to contain the SQL, but `/order.id` is a fine way to say
"order followed by any separator followed by id" when you want
`order.id`, `order_id` and `orderId`.

The `/e` offset and `ciw` are chapter 12 and chapter 10 doing their
jobs. Landing on the end of the match and changing the word you are on
is a pair you will use over and over: the pattern gets you to the right
place, the text object does the edit without you having to know how
long the word is.

@start refund.service.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { DatabaseClient } from '../database/database.client';
import { GatewayClient } from './gateway.client';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  constructor(
    @inject(DatabaseClient) protected databaseClient: DatabaseClient,
    @inject(GatewayClient) protected gatewayClient: GatewayClient,
  ) {}

  public async totalsByOrder(orderId: string) {
    return this.databaseClient.query(
      'SELECT order_id, SUM(amount) AS refund_total FROM refunds WHERE order_id = ? GROUP BY order_id',
      [orderId],
    );
  }

  public async exceedsOrder(orderId: string) {
    const rows = await this.databaseClient.query(
      'SELECT refund_total, order_total FROM order_totals WHERE order_id = ?',
      [orderId],
    );

    if (!rows.length) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return rows[0].refund_total > rows[0].order_total;
  }

  public async submit(refund: { id: string; orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const order = await this.gatewayClient.lookup(refund.orderId);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (await this.exceedsOrder(order.id)) {
      throw exception('REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL');
    }

    await this.gatewayClient.refund(order.id, refund.total);

    return { refundId: refund.id, submitted: true };
  }
}

@expected refund.service.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { DatabaseClient } from '../database/database.client';
import { GatewayClient } from './gateway.client';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  constructor(
    @inject(DatabaseClient) protected databaseClient: DatabaseClient,
    @inject(GatewayClient) protected gatewayClient: GatewayClient,
  ) {}

  public async totalsByOrder(orderId: string) {
    return this.databaseClient.query(
      'SELECT order_id, SUM(amount) AS refund_total FROM refunds WHERE order_id = ? GROUP BY order_id',
      [orderId],
    );
  }

  public async exceedsOrder(orderId: string) {
    const rows = await this.databaseClient.query(
      'SELECT refund_total, order_total FROM order_totals WHERE order_id = ?',
      [orderId],
    );

    if (!rows.length) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return rows[0].refund_total > rows[0].order_total;
  }

  public async submit(refund: { id: string; orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const order = await this.gatewayClient.lookup(refund.orderId);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (await this.exceedsOrder(order.id)) {
      throw exception('REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL');
    }

    await this.gatewayClient.refund(order.id, refund.amount);

    return { refundId: refund.id, submitted: true };
  }
}
